# Why PHP's `mail()` Function Sends Emails to Spam

Sending emails directly from a server using PHP's built-in `mail()` function is a common pattern for legacy applications, but it is fundamentally unreliable for modern email clients like Gmail. When recipients consistently land in the spam folder, the issue is rarely the logic inside your application code; it is almost always the reputation of the sending infrastructure.

The core problem lies in how PHP's `mail()` function operates by default. It passes the message directly to the server's Mail Transfer Agent (MTA), which then attempts to deliver it via SMTP. If you are running this on a shared hosting environment or a bare-metal VPS without dedicated email configuration, your IP address likely lacks an established reputation. Major providers like Google and Microsoft scan incoming mail against blacklists and evaluate several key factors before accepting the message.

Your current implementation has specific headers that trigger immediate suspicion:

```php
$headers = 'MIME-Version: 1.0' . "\r\n";
$headers .= 'Content-type: text/html; charset=iso-8859-1' . "\r\n";
$headers .= 'From: successive.testing@gmail.com' . "\r\n";
```

First, you are sending from a Gmail address (`successive.testing@gmail.com`). Google's Terms of Service strictly prohibit using personal or free-tier accounts for mass mail transmission or automated notifications. Their systems actively detect and block traffic originating from known spam traps associated with free domains. This alone is sufficient to flag the email as spam.

Second, your charset declarations are inconsistent. You define `Content-type` as `iso-8859-1`, yet your meta tags specify `windows-1252` and `windows-1252` again in the HTML head. While browsers often handle these gracefully, email protocols like SPF and DKIM rely on consistent metadata to verify sender authenticity. Mismatched or missing DNS records (SPF, DKIM, DMARC) cause receiving servers to reject the message or move it to the junk folder immediately.

Third, there is no authentication header. The `mail()` function does not automatically attach X-Verified-Sender or similar headers that prove the email came from a legitimate source. Without SPF and DKIM records configured in your DNS to match the sending domain, Gmail assumes the message is spoofed.

To resolve this, you cannot rely on tweaking headers within the PHP code alone. The solution requires moving away from `mail()` for transactional emails. Instead, you should use an SMTP client that supports authentication, such as PHPMailer or SwiftMailer. These libraries handle the SMTP handshake and allow you to authenticate with a dedicated email service provider (like SendGrid, Mailgun, or Amazon SES) using your own custom domain.

Here is how the logic shifts when using a proper SMTP library:

```php
use PHPMailer\PHPMailer\PHPMailer;
use PHPMailer\PHPMailer\Exception;

require 'vendor/autoload.php';

$mail = new PHPMailer(true);

try {
    // Use a dedicated sending domain, not @gmail.com
    $mail->isSMTP();
    $mail->Host       = 'smtp.example.com'; 
    $mail->SMTPAuth   = true;
    $mail->Username   = 'your-email@example.com';
    $mail->Password   = 'your-app-password';
    $mail->SMTPSecure = PHPMailer::ENCRYPTION_SMTPS;
    $mail->Port       = 465;

    $mail->setFrom('noreply@example.com', 'Support Team');
    $mail->addAddress('dineshnagarscriet@gmail.com');

    $mail->isHTML(true);
    $mail->Subject = 'Request for Department transfer';
    // Content remains the same HTML string
    $mail->Body    = '<div><b>' . htmlspecialchars($ticketDetail[0]['ticket_number']) . '</b></div>...';

    $mail->send();
} catch (Exception $e) {
    echo "Error sending email: {$mail->ErrorInfo}";
}
```

By authenticating with a dedicated SMTP provider, you gain access to tools that manage your domain's reputation. You can set up SPF and DKIM records for your custom domain, ensuring that Gmail recognizes the emails as legitimate. For high-volume applications, this architectural shift is non-negotiable. If you need robust email handling within a Laravel ecosystem, consider leveraging packages or services designed to integrate seamlessly with these standards.
